# [Diagnose common failures]($heading.id('diagnose-common-failures'))

Follow the checks in order until you find the first failure. Use the host and
port from your launch command. The examples use `127.0.0.1:8000` and, for Docker,
the container name `llmd` from [Deploy LLMD]($link.page('llmd/howto/deploy-llmd')).

```=html
<div class="readiness-path" data-readiness-path>
  <p class="readiness-shortcuts">
    <a href="#unknown-model">Unknown model</a>
    <a href="#metrics-dictionary">Metrics dictionary</a>
  </p>
  <ol class="readiness-steps" data-readiness-controls aria-label="Readiness checks" data-pagefind-ignore hidden>
    <li><button type="button" data-readiness-step="container" aria-controls="readiness-container" aria-pressed="false"><strong>1. Container</strong><small>Startup logs</small></button></li>
    <li><button type="button" data-readiness-step="listener" aria-controls="readiness-listener" aria-pressed="false"><strong>2. Listener</strong><small>GET /health</small></button></li>
    <li><button type="button" data-readiness-step="model" aria-controls="readiness-model" aria-pressed="false"><strong>3. Served model</strong><small>GET /v1/models</small></button></li>
    <li><button type="button" data-readiness-step="inference" aria-controls="readiness-inference" aria-pressed="false"><strong>4. Inference</strong><small>Short request</small></button></li>
  </ol>

  <section id="readiness-container" data-readiness-stage="container" aria-labelledby="readiness-container-title">
    <div class="readiness-stage-heading">
      <h3 id="readiness-container-title">Container</h3>
      <p>LLMD stays running and can access its model files and accelerator. For a native launch, check the LLMD process and its terminal output.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="readiness-layout">
      <div class="readiness-choices" data-readiness-choices data-pagefind-ignore hidden></div>
      <div class="readiness-procedures">
        <section id="model-access-fails" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="model-access-fails-title">
          <h4 id="model-access-fails-title">Model access fails</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Read the model-loading error in the <a href="#check-startup-and-readiness">startup logs</a>. Check the URI, revision, and credentials, or check the local files and mount.</dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>LLMD can read all files for the selected model revision.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Confirm <a href="../../reference/environment-variables/#gated-model-access">gated model access</a> and pass the token, or correct the <a href="../../reference/model-and-listener-flags/#local-model-files">local model directory</a> and bind mount.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section id="device-unavailable" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="device-unavailable-title">
          <h4 id="device-unavailable-title">Device unavailable</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Check <a href="#inspect-accelerator-usage">device visibility on the host</a>. With CUDA in Docker, run the <a href="https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/container-toolkit/latest/sample-workload.html">NVIDIA Container Toolkit sample workload</a> to check container access.</dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>The selected accelerator is visible on the host and accessible to the LLMD runtime.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Verify the host driver, image backend, and Docker device options against <a href="../../reference/hardware-compatibility/">Hardware compatibility</a>.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section id="startup-out-of-memory" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="startup-out-of-memory-title">
          <h4 id="startup-out-of-memory-title">Out of memory during startup</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Compare the weights at the selected precision with available device memory. In Docker, also check the container exit state.
              <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy Docker OOM check">docker inspect --format '{{.State.OOMKilled}}' llmd</code></pre>
            </dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>The weights fit in device memory. If <code>OOMKilled</code> is <code>true</code>, Docker recorded a container memory kill.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Use a smaller compatible model if device memory is insufficient. For a container memory kill, check the container’s memory limit and available host memory.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="readiness-listener" data-readiness-stage="listener" aria-labelledby="readiness-listener-title">
    <div class="readiness-stage-heading">
      <h3 id="readiness-listener-title">Listener</h3>
      <p><code>GET /health</code> returns <code>OK</code> on the host and port your client uses.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="readiness-layout">
      <div class="readiness-choices" data-readiness-choices data-pagefind-ignore hidden></div>
      <div class="readiness-procedures">
        <section id="connection-refused" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="connection-refused-title">
          <h4 id="connection-refused-title">Connection refused or health check times out</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd><pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy health check">curl --max-time 5 -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/health</code></pre></dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>The HTTP handler returns <code>OK</code>.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Let model loading and compilation finish. For Docker, confirm the published host port with <code>docker port llmd 8000/tcp</code> if the container listens on port 8000. If startup failed, resolve the first error in the <a href="#check-startup-and-readiness">startup logs</a>.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section id="port-in-use" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="port-in-use-title">
          <h4 id="port-in-use-title">Port already in use</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Read the bind error from LLMD or Docker to identify the address and port already in use.</dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>The selected host port is free and the health request succeeds after startup.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Choose a free <strong>Host port</strong> under <strong>Launch options</strong> in <a href="../../tutorial/start/">Start LLMD on any platform</a>. Use it for the model check and API requests.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="readiness-model" data-readiness-stage="model" aria-labelledby="readiness-model-title">
    <div class="readiness-stage-heading">
      <h3 id="readiness-model-title">Served model</h3>
      <p><code>GET /v1/models</code> lists the model ID your client should send.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="readiness-layout">
      <div class="readiness-choices" data-readiness-choices data-pagefind-ignore hidden></div>
      <div class="readiness-procedures">
        <section id="unknown-model" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="unknown-model-title">
          <h4 id="unknown-model-title">Request names an unknown model</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Query the same server and port your client used.
              <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy GET /v1/models">curl --max-time 5 -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models</code></pre>
            </dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd><code>data[0].id</code> contains the served model ID. For the deployment example, it is <code>lfm3</code>.
              <span class="command-language">JSON · Example response</span>
              <pre><code class="json" data-copy-label="Copy example models response">{ "data": [{ "id": "lfm3" }] }</code></pre>
            </dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Use that value verbatim as the <code>model</code> field in your request, for example <code>"model": "lfm3"</code>. The <a href="../../reference/models/#read-the-model-id">Models API reference</a> explains how <code>--model-name</code> sets the served ID.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="readiness-inference" data-readiness-stage="inference" aria-labelledby="readiness-inference-title">
    <div class="readiness-stage-heading">
      <h3 id="readiness-inference-title">Inference</h3>
      <p>A short request supported by the loaded model returns a valid result before you route traffic to the instance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="readiness-layout">
      <div class="readiness-choices" data-readiness-choices data-pagefind-ignore hidden></div>
      <div class="readiness-procedures">
        <section id="context-length-exceeded" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="context-length-exceeded-title">
          <h4 id="context-length-exceeded-title">400 with <code>context_length_exceeded</code></h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Compare the tokenized conversation, including the chat template, with the configured context length.</dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>The complete prompt is shorter than the context limit.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Shorten the conversation and retry the request. See <a href="../../reference/chat-completions/#errors">Chat completion errors</a>.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section id="requests-out-of-memory" data-readiness-symptom aria-labelledby="requests-out-of-memory-title">
          <h4 id="requests-out-of-memory-title">Requests exhaust memory after startup</h4>
          <dl class="readiness-checks">
            <dt>First check</dt>
            <dd>Watch <code>requests_rejected_oom</code> alongside request sizes and device usage while replaying the workload.
              <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy metrics check">curl --max-time 5 -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/metrics</code></pre>
            </dd>
            <dt>Expected result</dt>
            <dd>The rejection counter stays flat while those requests run.</dd>
            <dt>Recovery</dt>
            <dd>Reduce concurrency or context length and retest the same workload. See <a href="../../reference/batching-and-memory/">Batching and memory</a>.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
```

# [Check startup and readiness]($heading.id('check-startup-and-readiness'))

Inspect the container, its exit state, and its latest startup logs.

```sh
docker container ls -a --filter 'name=^/llmd$'
docker inspect --format '{{.State.Status}} exit={{.State.ExitCode}} oom={{.State.OOMKilled}}' llmd
docker logs --timestamps --tail 200 llmd
```

Follow the startup logs with `docker logs --follow llmd`. When Docker runs
LLMD with `--rm`, capture the startup output before Docker removes the exited
container. Keep a stopped container while investigating its exit state and
logs. See [Docker logs](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/logs/).

Check the HTTP listener and advertised model once startup has completed.

```sh
curl --max-time 5 -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
curl --max-time 5 -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models
```

`/health` returns `OK` when its handler responds. Check the expected model ID in
`/v1/models`, then send a [short chat request]($link.page('llmd/reference/chat-completions').ref('create-a-chat-completion')) and verify
that it generates a reply before routing traffic to the instance.

# [Scrape service metrics]($heading.id('scrape-service-metrics'))

LLMD exposes Prometheus metrics at `/metrics`. Inspect the raw export before
using metric names in a dashboard or alert.

```sh
curl --max-time 5 -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/metrics
```

For Prometheus running directly on the LLMD host, add a job under
`scrape_configs` in [its configuration](https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/configuration/#scrape_config).

```yaml
scrape_configs:
  - job_name: llmd
    metrics_path: /metrics
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['127.0.0.1:8000']
```

For a separate scraper, use a target reachable from its network and restrict
access to the metrics route to operators.

## [Metrics dictionary]($heading.id('metrics-dictionary'))

| Metric in the reviewed LLMD source | What to check |
| --- | --- |
| `requests_waiting`, `requests_running` | Queue depth and the number of requests already scheduled. |
| `request_queue_time_seconds` | Time from entering the queue to first scheduling. |
| `gen_ai_server_time_to_first_token` | Server time to first token, in seconds. |
| `gen_ai_server_request_duration` | End-to-end server request duration, in seconds. |
| `output_tokens_total` | Completion tokens emitted. Use a rate over time to measure throughput. |
| `requests_finished_total` | Finished enqueued requests by outcome. Use `reason="error"` to inspect failures during generation. |
| `requests_rejected_oom` | Requests the server rejected to avoid running out of memory. |

For a job named `llmd`, use this [PromQL rate expression](https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/functions/#rate)
for average output tokens per second by served model over five minutes.

```promql
sum by (model_name) (rate(output_tokens_total{job="llmd"}[5m]))
```

Validate the series and labels against `/metrics` for the deployed release.
Use the queue depth alongside latency and throughput. A growing queue with
longer waits indicates that requests are reaching the service faster than it
can schedule them.

# [Inspect accelerator usage]($heading.id('inspect-accelerator-usage'))

Use [zml-smi](https://zml.ai/posts/zml-smi/) on a supported host to list
devices, and run `zml-smi --top` to view available utilization, memory, and
temperature readings. With CUDA, `nvidia-smi` provides a host-side device and
driver check. Compare those readings with the LLMD queue and token metrics for
the same time window. See [Environment variables]($link.page('llmd/reference/environment-variables'))
for runtime diagnostics.

# [Record the failure]($heading.id('record-the-failure'))

Record the LLMD version, backend tag, model revision, accelerator and
driver, model ID, relevant timestamps, and the exact error before changing
settings. Remove tokens, credential values, and sensitive prompt or output
text from shared logs. The commands and metrics above are source-reviewed.
Live checks against a deployed LLMD instance remain pending.
